//! Burntime's first playable slice, on AROS.
//!
//! Everything it needs is compiled in: `packer` decodes the artwork on the host
//! and writes `burntime.pack`, so this side needs no PNG decoder and touches no
//! file system. The rules come from `burngfx::game`, unchanged — the same code
//! the host tests run against.
//!
//!     cargo run --release          # from this directory, into AROS via QEMU
//!     cargo run                    # on the host, same program
//!
//! On the world map you are *at* a location, and clicking another sets out
//! down a road — the days come from `gam.dat`'s own graph, only a direct
//! neighbour is a destination, and a journey cannot be redirected once begun.
//! Clicking the location you are standing at goes inside it.
//!
//! Choosing takes two clicks. The first shows the road's length beside how
//! long the character's food and water will last; the second sets out. Both
//! the panel and the departure ask [`burngfx::game::depart`], so the panel
//! cannot offer a journey the rules would refuse.
//!
//! Inside, click to walk. Arriving on a green rectangle opens the room — the 1993
//! interior out of `raum_N.pac`, with the room's name under it. Escape closes
//! the room, then leaves the location, then quits; `i` puts the old debug
//! panel back over the picture.
//!
//! The left of a room is where the original's inventory panel sits, and that
//! is what is drawn there: six slots for what the character carries, against
//! the room's own slots on the right. Clicking one moves the item across.
//!
//! One item is placed at the start of a game, on the floor of Muarab's
//! entrance. That was scaffolding; a room's contents are generated at the
//! start of a game since (`burngfx::spawn`).
//!
//! Right-click an item to eat it, in either grid — the original allows both.
//! A snake is `food=7`; its `heal=28` is what a doctor charges for it, not
//! what a meal restores.
//!
//! Right-click an empty bottle at a spring to fill it, and a full one to
//! drink it; both keep their slot. Filling is all or nothing — the spring
//! must hold a whole container's worth.
//!
//! `n` ends the day. There is no clock: a day passes when you say so, and
//! then springs yield, a character with nothing left eats what they carry,
//! going without costs health, and the day's food and water are spent. The
//! order is [`burngfx::game::character_day`]'s and it is the original's.
//!
//! On the host, `walk` starts with the collision overlay on and
//! `shot <path.png>` writes one frame and exits. The second exists so that
//! showing what the program draws never means photographing the screen it is
//! drawn on — the window may not be the only thing there.
//!
//! `do x,y x,y ...` replays those clicks first, each one waiting for the
//! previous walk to finish, so `shot` can photograph a state several moves in
//! without anyone touching the mouse. `do 160,200 esc` also accepts `esc` for
//! the escape key. Its coordinates are **map** coordinates, converted to the
//! window on the way in, so a script does not change meaning when the camera
//! moves.

#![cfg_attr(target_os = "aros", no_std)]
#![cfg_attr(target_os = "aros", no_main)]
#![cfg_attr(target_os = "aros", feature(core_float_math))]

